./packages/lararium-sensorium/fixtures/claude-week-2026-10-07/sources/claude/7bdba225-5131-4cfb-a258-6d3e7da875f9/subagents/agent-a876d2adbe9a4390a.jsonl:1:{"parentUuid":null,"isSidechain":true,"promptId":"a474019c-acfa-471e-8c33-89e46680e0d2","agentId":"a876d2adbe9a4390a","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":"BLIND READER — consolidate two opposed surveys into ONE plan. You have no access to the repository they describe, by design: you cannot be sentimental about a tool you never used, and every claim you make must stand on what the reports say.\n\n═══ WHAT YOU HAVE ═══\nFour files, all under `/home/joshu/.claude/jobs/86a17260/tmp/trade/`:\n- `yin-wave1-for-yang.md` — the YIN pole's first survey (what can COLLAPSE)\n- `yang-wave1-for-yin.md` — the YANG pole's first survey (what must STAY DISTINCT, and what nothing reads)\n- `yin-wave2.md` — YIN's second pass, after reading YANG's findings\n- `yang-wave2.md` — YANG's second pass, after reading YIN's findings\nRead all four. The two wave-one files are my faithful paraphrase of the poles' own reports; the wave-two files are closer to their own words. Every number in them is the pole's measurement, attributed to that pole.\n\nDO NOT read or write anything in `/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine` or any git repository. Do not run gates, tests or builds. Do not edit any tracked file anywhere. Write only inside `/home/joshu/.claude/jobs/86a17260/tmp/fable/` (create it). No helper agents, no Monitor watchers.\n\n═══ THE SUBJECT, as the reports describe it ═══\nA VS Code TextMate grammar for TiddlyWiki5 wikitext plus a `memetic-wikitext` dialect. The grammar has SETTLED; the infrastructure around it grew by accretion: 49 gates over 35 instruments, 39 invariant test files, 20 declaration files under `corpus/`, 40 pinned tokenization snapshots, 87–89 hand-written assertion fixtures, 82 npm scripts, and a record (CHANGELOG, a ~1840-line LEDGER, MIGRATION, README, SCOPE-NAMES, docs) carrying roughly a hundred unguarded figures.\n\n═══ THE GOAL, in the operator's own words ═══\n\"Consolidate, isomorphic-by-intent, YIN collapse the TESTING harness … the rest of the infrastructure has YANG exploded and needs a deep Aftermath/Evergreen & L-Prime pass on both test-harness and docs.\"\nRead that as four things, and keep them distinct in your plan:\n1. **Isomorphic by intent** — ONE SHAPE PER INTENT, named once. Where two things answer one question by different means, they fuse; where two things answer different questions that merely look alike, they stay and the record says why.\n2. **YIN collapse** — fewer moving parts, and every part that remains earning its place.\n3. **Aftermath / Evergreen** — the record states what HOLDS, not what used to; a dated reading carries its date; a live claim is derivable.\n4. **L-Prime** — prose that names what a thing DOES rather than asserting what it IS, in the harness comments and the docs both.\n\n═══ WHAT I WANT FROM YOU ═══\nA single consolidation plan, written for the hand that will enact it. Specifically:\n\n**A. The intent map.** Group every instrument, declaration surface, fixture family and script the reports name under the QUESTION it answers. One group per question. Within each group, say which members are one shape and which are genuinely different grains — the poles give you axes (population, scope, well-formedness, reader, grain, authority). Where the reports disagree about a group, say which pole's evidence you follow and why.\n\n**B. The ordered plan.** Every item the poles agreed on, every item they split on, ordered so that no step depends on a step after it. For each: what changes, what is lost, what must exist first, and which pole's measurement supports it. Both poles independently ranked one item first — the shared-`owedOf` fix, where two gates read one ledger with incompatible predicates and one added word makes a gate unable to fail. Say whether you agree with that ordering and why. Both poles also converged on a single underlying defect — no syntax distinguishing a dated reading from a live claim. Decide where that sits in your order.\n\n**C. The adjudications.** The poles disagree, or one concedes against its own interest, in several places: the fixture collapse (50 vs 60, and whether vocabulary subsumption is a safe criterion at all), the ward's `.test.js` exclusion, `overreach-cut`'s CI reachability, the debt backlogs and the one-cause ratchet, the colour cluster's irreproducible header integers. Rule on each, on the evidence in front of you, and name what would change your ruling.\n\n**D. The Evergreen and L-Prime pass, scoped as work rather than as a wish.** The reports give you copula densities per file, archaeology concentrations, four wrong present-tense counts, and one file whose copulas sit inside a verbatim quotation that an L-Prime pass would destroy. Say what the pass touches, what it must not touch, and how a hand would know the difference.\n\n**E. What you refuse.** Items that look obvious and are not — the reports flag deliberate absences recorded as refusals, two throwaway scouts that are the only implementation of a reading, and prose that is the only thing holding a law. Name every one you would protect from a tidy-up, and the line a future hand should read before touching it.\n\n**F. What neither pole measured** that your plan depends on, and what would settle it.\n\nWrite plainly. Attribute every number to the pole that measured it, and never invent one. Where the two poles' numbers differ (50 against 60 fixtures; 330/93 against 319/92 owed rows; 44-of-65 against 65-of-65 containers), say so and say which difference matters to the plan. Your report is the thing the enacting hand will follow, so prefer a short defensible plan over a long speculative one. Save it to `/home/joshu/.claude/jobs/86a17260/tmp/fable/consolidation-plan.md` as well as returning it."},"uuid":"8e889374-9a67-4d10-8f90-e249b55c832e","timestamp":"2026-10-07T16:32:17.737Z","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/VSCode-TW5-Syntax","sessionId":"7bdba225-5131-4cfb-a258-6d3e7da875f9","version":"2.1.277","gitBranch":"release/3.0.0","slug":"luminous-strolling-torvalds"}
