./bags/lararium/ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/dreamnet-prior-art.mem:520:DreamNet explicitly rejects shared implementation as the interoperability surface. ActivityPub uses both. DreamNet's approach appears more philosophically consistent but harder to bootstrap: new implementations must correctly interpret shared vocabulary without reference to a canonical implementation. This requires excellent SharktoothSigil specification and strong test fixtures.
./bags/lararium/ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/dreamnet-prior-art.mem:577:''Description'': DreamNet principals use keypairs (did:key or equivalent) plus CIDs. These identifiers provide unforgeability but not human readability. No named mechanism gives users human-readable names for other vessels, cabalGroups, or genesis artifacts. The SharktoothSigil vocabulary may fill part of this gap but lacks description as a petname system.
./bags/lararium/ha.ka.ba/lararium/api/hydration-grain.mem:46:''Read the column and the integrity shows.'' Each grain answers the same question — //what may a holder know without paying for the whole?// — and each answers it in its own layer's vocabulary. ''The vocabularies MUST NOT merge'' (they name different layers, and a merged word would flatten the layer distinction the model rests on); ''the integrity MUST stay legible across them'' (a reader who sees three unrelated mechanisms has lost the wave).
./bags/lararium/ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/realm-nexus-unblur-scout.mem:49:So the two ''PLANES'' already stand correctly separated: the public boards derive off `nexusRegistryUri`/`nexusHandlesUri`/`CROSSROADS_DOC_URI`, the private realm doc off `REALM_DOC_URI`. Only the ''WORDS'' cross-cut. That reads as good news for the ruling: it asks for a vocabulary sweep over an architecture that already sorts.
./bags/lararium/ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/realm-nexus-unblur-scout.mem:56:Different shapes, one word, adjacent files. This reads as a latent ''correctness'' seam rather than a naming one, and an unblur pass MUST resolve it before touching vocabulary.
