./bags/lararium/ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/lamplighters.mem:444:* ''Nothing in the field welds place-anchored authority to term-bounded office.'' The nearest misses: CT's `previous_operators` (resolves the office-holder ''at the time of the act'', yet only to attribute), Kubernetes bound ServiceAccount tokens (term = the life of a place, no succession), DNSSEC root TCRs (real term-bounded office, ''procedural and legal, never cryptographic''), and SPKI §7.7 (held the whole vocabulary in 1999, shipped nothing).
./bags/lararium/ha.ka.ba/lararium/api/residency-model.mem:50:# ''RDF named graphs + SPARQL Update'' (W3C 2013) — the quad `(s, p, o, g)` makes "the same triple in multiple graphs" first-class. SPARQL Update settled the action verb vocabulary in 2013 after twenty years of practice.
./bags/lararium/ha.ka.ba/lararium/api/residency-model.mem:161:This dual-surface design — ACTION verbs for the //gesture//, archival verbs for the //audit// — resonates structurally with web3 causal-islands models. The archival profession solved the identity-across-custody problem before software did; we adopt the discipline along with the vocabulary. The ''Floating Librarians of Mu register'' applies — Mu's stewards of memory cooperate with archivists, not with version-control engineers.
./bags/lararium/ha.ka.ba/lararium/api/residency-model.mem:175:# ''Verb vocabulary from set-algebra + cataloging.'' The //residency// ACTION verbs SHOULD draw from SPARQL Update; effect annotations SHOULD draw from archival practice. The surface MAY also carry ''non-residency'' verbs that ride the same rail (#/non-residency-verbs). The version-control ban holds across the whole surface: verbs from VCS (stage/commit/push, branch/merge, cherry-pick) MUST NOT enter — they import a timeline mental model that does not fit. A non-residency verb that //folds// state (RECONCILE) MUST stay ''conflict-SURFACING, never merge-arbitrating'' (#/conflict-resolution), or it reads as a banned merge verb.
./bags/lararium/ha.ka.ba/lararium/api/residency-model.mem:279:* ''RDF Named Graphs + SPARQL Update'' — Carroll/Bizer/Hayes/Stickler 2005; W3C RDF 1.1 §4 (2013). The settled vocabulary for "same triple in multiple graphs."
